+---
+id: lem-qftx-gaga-euclidean-finite-order-refinement
+kind: lemma
+title: "Locally finite refinements of Euclidean open covers have order at most the dimension plus one"
+status: draft
+origin: pipeline
+domain: mathematics
+library: mathematics
+physical_scope: "A topological theorem about Euclidean open sets; no physical model or empirical claim is involved."
+dependency_level: 0
+deps:
+  - def-axiom-of-choice
+  - def-metric-space
+dependency_roles:
+  def-axiom-of-choice: mathematical-premise
+  def-metric-space: mathematical-premise
+justified_by: []
+forward_refs: []
+aliases: []
+proof_strategy: direct
+provenance:
+  statement: literature-derived
+  proof: ai-generated
+sources:
+  references:
+    - title: "Ian Agol, Complex Analysis and Riemann Surfaces, course notes"
+      url: "https://math.berkeley.edu/~ianagol/complexriemann.pdf"
+      locator: "§6, Theorem 6.5, printed/PDF p. 29: the sheaf-dimension vanishing theorem is stated from fine covers with empty (m+2)-fold intersections; the refinement construction is supplied in this item."
+    - title: "Jean-Pierre Demailly, Analytic Methods in Algebraic Geometry"
+      url: "https://www-fourier.univ-grenoble-alpes.fr/~demailly/manuscripts/agbook.pdf"
+      locator: "Chapter IX §4.B, printed/PDF p. 421: the local acyclicity proof uses topological dimension at most real dimension; this item proves the needed Euclidean refinement bound directly."
+pipeline_run: advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23
+---
+
+## Statement
+
+Assume full AC. Let $m\ge1$ and let $\Omega$ be a nonempty open subset of
+$\mathbb R^m$ with its Euclidean metric. For every open cover $\mathcal U$ of
+$\Omega$, there is a locally finite open cover $\mathcal V$ and a refinement
+map $\rho:\mathcal V\to\mathcal U$ with $V\subseteq\rho(V)$ for every
+$V\in\mathcal V$, such that each point of $\Omega$ lies in at most $m+1$
+members of $\mathcal V$. Here locally finite means that every point has an
+open neighborhood meeting only finitely many members, and the order bound
+means the stated pointwise multiplicity bound.
+
+## Statement references
+
+[Agol, §6, Theorem 6.5, p. 29](https://math.berkeley.edu/~ianagol/complexriemann.pdf)
+
+[Demailly, Chapter IX §4.B, p. 421](https://www-fourier.univ-grenoble-alpes.fr/~demailly/manuscripts/agbook.pdf)
+
+## Facts & Assumptions
+
+**Given:** Full AC, $m\ge1$, a nonempty open set $\Omega\subseteq\mathbb R^m$,
+and an arbitrary open cover $\mathcal U$ of $\Omega$.
+
+[A1] [[def-axiom-of-choice]]: every family of nonempty sets has a choice function.
+
+[F1] [[def-metric-space]]: the Euclidean distance satisfies positivity, symmetry, and the triangle inequality.
+
+## Proof
+
+**Proof technique:** direct.
+
+1.1 For $x\in\Omega$ define
+$$
+\delta(x):=\sup_{U\in\mathcal U}\min\{1,\operatorname{dist}(x,\mathbb R^m\setminus U)\},\qquad
+\varepsilon(x):=\min\{1,\operatorname{dist}(x,\mathbb R^m\setminus\Omega),\delta(x)\}.
+$$
+The conventions $\operatorname{dist}(x,\varnothing)=+\infty$ and $\min\{1,+\infty\}=1$ cover the case $U=\mathbb R^m$ or $\Omega=\mathbb R^m$. Since $\mathcal U$ covers $\Omega$ and each $U$ is open, $\delta(x)>0$. For any nonempty closed set $E$, the triangle inequality gives $\operatorname{dist}(x,E)\le|x-y|+\operatorname{dist}(y,E)$ by taking the infimum over $E$, and interchanging $x,y$ gives the reverse bound; capping at $1$ preserves this $1$-Lipschitz estimate. The supremum of a family of $1$-Lipschitz functions is $1$-Lipschitz because $f_U(x)\le f_U(y)+|x-y|$ for every $U$, hence $\sup_Uf_U(x)\le\sup_Uf_U(y)+|x-y|$, and likewise after interchanging $x,y$. The minimum of two $1$-Lipschitz functions satisfies the same estimate, so $\varepsilon$ is $1$-Lipschitz. For each $x$, the definition of the supremum supplies $U_x\in\mathcal U$ with $\min\{1,\operatorname{dist}(x,\mathbb R^m\setminus U_x)\}>\delta(x)/2$, hence $B(x,\delta(x)/2)\subset U_x$. Also $\varepsilon(x)\le\operatorname{dist}(x,\mathbb R^m\setminus\Omega)$. [A1, F1, given, construct]
+
+1.2 Let $\mathcal Q$ be the family of all closed dyadic cubes $Q\subset\Omega$ satisfying
+$$\operatorname{diam}(Q)<\frac1{16}\inf_{y\in Q}\varepsilon(y).$$
+Every point of $\Omega$ belongs to such a cube at sufficiently fine dyadic scale, because $\varepsilon$ is continuous and positive there. Each qualifying cube has diameter $<1/16$, so along the nested dyadic ancestors of any such cube only finitely many can qualify; choose a maximal qualifying ancestor. The maximal cubes cover $\Omega$ and have pairwise disjoint interiors, since two dyadic cubes with intersecting interiors are nested and a contained qualifying cube cannot be maximal. [step 1.1, construct]
+
+2.1 The maximal cubes are locally finite. Fix $x\in\Omega$, put $a=\varepsilon(x)>0$, and choose $r=a/8$. If a maximal cube $Q$ meets $B(x,r)$ and has side length $s<a/(512\sqrt m)$, its dyadic parent $P$ has diameter $2\sqrt m\,s<a/256$. Every $y\in P$ then satisfies $|y-x|<a/8+a/256<a/4$, so $P\subset\Omega$ and the Lipschitz bound gives $\varepsilon(y)>3a/4$ on $P$. Thus $\operatorname{diam}(P)<a/256<(3a/4)/16\le\inf_P\varepsilon/16$, so $P$ also qualifies, contradicting maximality of $Q$. Hence each maximal cube meeting $B(x,r)$ has side at least $a/(512\sqrt m)$. All such cubes lie in the bounded set $B(x,r+1/16)$ and their centers are separated by at least this lower bound in the maximum norm, because their interiors are disjoint axis-parallel dyadic cubes. A bounded Euclidean box contains only finitely many points with a fixed positive separation, so only finitely many such cubes meet $B(x,r)$. [step 1.1, F1, algebra]
+
+2.2 Take the maximal cubes and all their faces, subdividing each face wherever a face of an adjacent maximal cube meets it. Only finitely many adjacent cubes meet any fixed cube, because it is compact and the family in step 2.1 is locally finite. The subdivisions match on common faces: axis-parallel dyadic intervals are nested or have disjoint interiors, so every intersection is a face in the induced common grid. The resulting closed cells are convex orthogonal polytopes, meet along whole faces, cover $\Omega$, and form a locally finite complex of dimension at most $m$. Triangulate this complex inductively by dimension. Having triangulated the boundary faces compatibly, choose an interior barycenter of each positive-dimensional convex cell and cone it to each boundary simplex; convexity makes the cones cover the cell and intersect along shared subfaces. The construction agrees on common faces because their boundary triangulations were fixed at earlier dimensions. Local finiteness of the cells gives local finiteness of the resulting simplices. This yields a locally finite simplicial complex of dimension at most $m$ with realization $\Omega$. [step 2.1, construct]
+
+3.1 For each vertex $v$ of this triangulation, let $\operatorname{st}(v)$ be the union of the relative interiors of simplices containing $v$. In the geometric realization it is the set where the barycentric coordinate of $v$ is positive, hence it is open. Every point lies in the star of each vertex of its unique minimal simplex, so these stars cover $\Omega$. If a point lies in the stars of $v_0,\ldots,v_k$, all these vertices have positive coordinates in the same minimal simplex. Its dimension is at most $m$, so $k+1\le m+1$. A neighborhood meeting only finitely many simplices meets stars of only their finitely many vertices; hence the star cover is locally finite and has order at most $m+1$. [step 2.2]
+
+3.2 If $y\in\operatorname{st}(v)$, then $y$ and $v$ lie in a simplex contained in a maximal cube $Q$ containing $v$. By step 1.2,
+$$|y-v|\le\operatorname{diam}(Q)<\varepsilon(v)/16\le\delta(v)/16<\delta(v)/2.$$
+Step 1.1 supplies $U_v\in\mathcal U$ with $B(v,\delta(v)/2)\subset U_v$, so $\operatorname{st}(v)\subset U_v$. Choosing one such $U_v$ for each vertex defines a refinement map $\rho$; AC [A1] supplies these simultaneous choices. The star cover is therefore a locally finite open refinement of $\mathcal U$ with the required order. [A1, F1, step 1.1, step 1.2, step 2.2, step 3.1] ∎
